tructure not limited by their  imagination  so what we do and the key innovation of  bigquery  is it's a serverless platform google  takes  care of the scaling the operations the  resiliency  it's a platform that you can load in  exabytes worth of data  and run petabyte scale queries over  hundreds of trillions of rows  and we automatically scale it and manage  it on your behalf  this means that when your quants want to  find interesting patterns  they don't have to make trade-offs as to  how much compute is available  and how many servers are being used  right now a key problem that we hear  from customers  is the team in london are okay during  the morning but then it comes to lunch  time when the new york team come online  and suddenly they get more competitive  towards the data warehouse  there's a fixed number of calls or a  fixed amount of disk  and then san francisco comes online and  they're further countries  and suddenly there's too many convenient  people computing for too few resources  and you end up with contention  and everything slows down and then you  revert back to the focus  is writing queries that fit the  infrastructure not queries add value  to your business underlying all of this  is the architectural difference around  bigquery we released a white paper on  the technology we call dremel inside  google that we developed 10 years ago  there's two key components of dremel on  the left hand side  is storage replicated distributed  storage with eleven nines of durability  we can store exabytes of data a hundred  percent of that is hot  online active and queryable it has the  same latency profile whether you're  querying data from an hour ago  or 15 years ago it's immediately  available for querying  the right hand side is a highly  available compute cluster  that we manage on behalf of our  customers  we make available a very large amount of  compute in multiple data centers around  the world  that can be span up on demand for each  particular qu