h storage do i make available  for my users  how much ram and compute do i provide  for my users  and ultimately you end up making a  trade-off do you build  a very large data warehouse and size it  for peak capacity  but end up running it when users aren't  accessing it or do you make it too small  such that there's too much contention  and users are fighting over a finite  number of resources  the difference with bigquery is we  manage that for you  bigquery is a serverless platform where  we run the software  the infrastructure the networking we  deal with the updates the operations and  the backup and everything  to ensure it's highly performant for  every user  refinitive have three core areas of  responsibility  data design that is designing the tables  and the layout of the data that users  interact with  data management getting the data into  bigquery  ensuring it's up to date and making sure  that it goes through the proper data  life cycle  and then finally access control sharing  the right data with the right end users  the final piece is customers their  responsibility is analyzing that data  getting the value that is their job to  extract from that data  so they don't have to think about  networking and cores and storage and the  etl process  they focus on doing their job i'm going  to hand back to catholic now to wrap us  up  thanks tom so as we've seen today it is  possible to access query ready data  without the need to store or manage a  real-time market data feed yourself  you can also leverage bigquery compute  and google cloud tools to analyze the  data  and not have to worry about provisioning  storage or compute  and finally you can also ensure that you  have access to an up-to-date  backed up and replicated data source  without the need for you to manage those  backups or disaster recovery instances  it's not just a matter of reducing the  additional eight dollars that are spent  on processing storing and transforming  the data  but it's also 