ery that comes in  in the middle linking this together is a  petabit scale  distributed memory tier very customized  low latency networking to link these  nodes together  what happens here is when a query comes  in  we analyze that query to work out the  exact right  size and shape of data warehouse needed  for that specific query at that specific  time  we know exactly where the data is how we  sliced it  sharded it partitioned it and indexed it  on your behalf  so we know how many machines we might  need to read that data in parallel  so we'll take a guess maybe it's a  hundred or a thousand to read the data  then we'll go through the different  phases in the query life cycle doing  grouping  aggregation and filtering more  dynamically  change the size of that cluster for you  to use the right amount of resources  to return the query as quickly as  possible  so this is automatic and this is managed  on your behalf  and every single user for every single  query  gets the experience as if a data  warehouse had been custom built for that  exact query every single time  and we spin this up virtually on the fly  for you  i'd like to talk briefly around the  shared responsibility model  where google cloud provides a benefit to  refinitive  who provides a benefit to our mutual  customers google cloud is externalizing  the infrastructure and data centers that  google has spent billions of dollars  building  we build and design our own physical  data centers  we build and design our own physical  servers and networking  and this is public and available some  great research out there for you to look  into  we take care of managing the physical  infrastructure on your behalf  and typically we see organizations  having two choices  they can consume infrastructure as a  service where they have access to  virtual machines in google cloud  they can take a third-party data  warehouse platform and they can spin it  up themselves  but the first question they need to ask  is how much storage do i make available  for my users  how much ram and compute do i provide  for my users  and ultimately you end up making a  trade-off do you build  a very large data warehouse and size it  for peak capacity  but end up running it when users aren't  accessing it or do you make it too small  such that there's too much contention  and users are fighting over a finite  number of resources  the difference with bigquery is we  manage that for you  bigquery is a serverless platform where  we run the software  the infrastructure the networking we  deal with the updates the operations and  the backup and everything  to ensure it's highly performant for  every user  refinitive have three core areas of  responsibility  data design that is designing the tables  and the layout of the data that users  interact with  data management getting the data into  bigquery  ensuring it's up to date and making sure  that it goes through the proper data  life cycle  and then finally access control sharing  the right data with the right end users  the final piece is customers their  responsibility is analyzing that data  getting the value that is their job to  extract from that data  so they don't have to think about  networking and cores and storage and the  etl process  they focus on doing their job i'm going  to hand back to catholic now to wrap us  up  thanks tom so as we've seen today it is  possible to access query ready data  without the need to store or manage a  real-time market data feed yourself  you can also leverage bigquery compute  and google cloud tools to analyze the  data  and not have to worry about provisioning  storage or compute  and finally you can also ensure that you  have access to an up-to-date  backed up and replicated data source  without the need for you to manage those  backups or disaster recovery instances  it's not just a matter of reducing the  additional eight dollars that are spent  on processing storing and transforming  the data  but it's also about reducing the time it  takes to enable  end users across your organization to  access the relevant data sets  and allow them to focus on where they  can really make a difference to your  business  whether that be the success of the  trading strategy  managing risk more effectively or  reducing costs of execution  so hope you enjoyed the session today  and from both of us  thanks very much for tuning in
 

